1. What is Pods:
    - A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
    - A pod usually contains:
        One container
        Shared network
        Shared storage
        Shared lifecycle
    - Pod Characteristics
        Each pod gets its own IP address
        Containers inside a pod communicate using localhost
        Pods are temporary (ephemeral)
        If a pod crashes, Kubernetes can recreate it
    - Why Pods Alone Are Not Enough
        - Pods are fragile because:
            They can die
            IP addresses change
            Scaling manually is difficult
        - This is why Deployments exist.
    - Example:
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: Pod
        metadata:
            name: nginx-pod
        spec:
            containers:
            - name: nginx
                image: nginx
                ports:
                - containerPort: 80

2. What is Deployments:
    - A Deployment manages pods automatically.
    - It provides:
        Scaling
        Self-healing
        Rolling updates
        Rollbacks
    - Instead of creating pods directly in production, you usually create Deployments.
    - Deployment Architecture
        Deployment
            ↓
        ReplicaSet
            ↓
        Pods
    - Example:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    metadata:
        name: nginx-deployment
    spec:
        replicas: 3
        selector:
            matchLabels:
            app: nginx
        template:
            metadata:
            labels:
                app: nginx
        spec:
            containers:
            - name: nginx
                image: nginx:latest
                ports:
                - containerPort: 80

3. What is Services:
    - Pods are dynamic:
        Pod IPs change
        Pods restart
        Pods scale up/down
    - A Service provides a stable network endpoint.
    - It acts like:
        A load balancer
        A stable DNS name
        A traffic router
    - Example:
        apiVersion: v1
        kind: Service
        metadata:
            name: nginx-service
        spec:
            selector:
                app: nginx
            ports:
            - protocol: TCP
                port: 80
                targetPort: 80
            type: ClusterIP

4. How Service Works:
    Users
        ↓
    Service
        ↓
    Pods
    - The service:
        Finds matching pods using labels
        Routes traffic automatically
        Load balances traffic

5. Service Types:
    - Kubernetes supports multiple service types.
    - ClusterIP (Default): Internal-only access.
        - Accessible:
            Only inside cluster
        - Use cases:
            Backend APIs
            Databases
            Internal microservices
    - NodePort: Exposes application on node IP + port.
        - Accessible externally: <NodeIP>:30000-32767
        - 192.168.1.10:30080
    - LoadBalancer: Creates cloud load balancer automatically.
        - Supported on:
            AWS
            Azure
            GCP
        - Best for production external apps.
    - ExternalName: Maps service to external DNS name.
        - Useful for:
            External databases
            Third-party APIs

6. Comparing Service Types
    Type	        Internal	    External	Use Case
    ClusterIP	    Yes	            No	        Internal communication
    NodePort	    Yes	            Yes	        Testing/dev
    LoadBalancer	Yes	            Yes	        Production
    ExternalName	External only	External	DNS mapping